    26	
    27	## Core Principles
    28	
    29	> **A hook that blocks Claude is worse than a missed record.**
    30	
    31	PostToolUse/PostToolUseFailure hooks MUST always exit 0. Diagnostics go to stderr; the script must never abort the tool call it is hooked to. Use a `bail()` function that logs and `exit 0`s gracefully. The `session-start.sh` is the exception — it exits 1 (fatal) for: the `$CLAUDE_ENV_FILE` env-file guard (unset or unwritable), an empty or missing stdin payload, and required-export failures under `set -euo pipefail` strict mode. Any of these makes the env-passthrough entirely unreliable, so exit 1 is correct.
    32	
    33	> **Strict mode, but scope it correctly.**
    34	
    35	`session-start.sh` uses `set -euo pipefail` because any failure in the env passthrough is fatal — there is no meaningful fallback. `post-tool-use-agents.sh` uses `set -uo pipefail` but NOT `-e`, because individual extraction steps may legitimately produce empty strings or non-zero exits that should not abort the whole hook.
    36	
    37	> **jq -r @sh for every JSON value written to a shell file.**
    38	
    39	When writing `export VAR=value` lines to `$CLAUDE_ENV_FILE`, always serialize via `jq -r '@sh "export VAR=\(.field)"'`. The `@sh` format produces POSIX single-quote quoting that is safe against paths with spaces, shell metacharacters, empty strings, single quotes, and Unicode. Never use raw string concatenation or printf for this purpose.
    40	
    41	> **flock -x serializes every read-modify-write on shared JSON files.**
    42	
    43	Any hook that reads, modifies, and writes a shared file (e.g., `session.json`) must hold an exclusive flock for the entire critical section. Release is automatic when the subshell exits. Validate the output JSON before the atomic `mv`; if validation fails, delete the tmp file and `exit 0` (do not replace the original with garbage).
    44	
    45	> **Tool-name scope filter before any real work.**
    46	
    47	Hooks registered for broad events (PostToolUse fires on every tool) MUST filter to the specific tool(s) they care about as the very first substantive check after stdin parsing. Use a `case` statement; unrecognized tool names must `exit 0` cleanly, not fall through to the hook logic.
    48	
    49	---
    50	
    51	## Procedures
    52	
    53	### P1 — Hook event registration in `.claude/settings.json`
    54	
    55	Register every hook under `hooks.<EventName>[]` in `.claude/settings.json`. Each entry must have:
    56	
    57	- `matcher`: a regex string matched against `tool_name` (for PostToolUse/PostToolUseFailure) or the top-level `source` field (for SessionStart). Use `|` for alternatives: `"Task|Agent"` matches both Task and Agent tool names. For SessionStart, the matcher is matched against the top-level `source` field (distinct from `hook_event_name`); the current project uses `"startup|resume|clear|compact"` to cover all SessionStart triggers.
    58	- `hooks[].type`: must be `"command"` — required field in every hook command object.
    59	- `hooks[].command`: the bare path to the hook script, e.g. `".claude/hooks/session-start.sh"` (no `bash ` prefix).
    60	
    61	Both PostToolUse and PostToolUseFailure entries can point to the same script (as `post-tool-use-agents.sh` does) — the hook reads `hook_event_name` from stdin to distinguish the event type.
    62	
    63	Example shape (from project settings):
    64	```json
    65	"SessionStart": [
    66	  {
    67	    "matcher": "startup|resume|clear|compact",
    68	    "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": ".claude/hooks/session-start.sh" }]
    69	  }
    70	],
    71	"PostToolUse": [
    72	  {
    73	    "matcher": "Task|Agent",
    74	    "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": ".claude/hooks/post-tool-use-agents.sh" }]
    75	  }
    76	],
    77	"PostToolUseFailure": [
    78	  {
    79	    "matcher": "Task|Agent",
    80	    "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": ".claude/hooks/post-tool-use-agents.sh" }]
    81	  }
    82	]
    83	```
    84	
    85	### P2 — stdin JSON payload structure
    86	
    87	Claude Code pipes a JSON object to stdin on every hook event. Always read it with `payload="$(cat)"` at the top of the script, then parse with `jq`. Never stream stdin directly into multiple `jq` calls — read once, use `<<<"$payload"` for all subsequent extractions.
    88	
    89	Common fields present across all events:
    90	- `session_id` — the current session UUID
    91	- `cwd` — the working directory at hook invocation
    92	- `hook_event_name` — the event name string (e.g., `"PostToolUse"`)
    93	- `transcript_path` — path to the session transcript JSONL
    94	
    95	PostToolUse/PostToolUseFailure also include:
    96	- `tool_name` — name of the tool that was used (e.g., `"Task"`, `"Agent"`)
    97	- `tool_use_id` — UUID that correlates the tool call across hook events and transcript entries
    98	- `tool_input` — the full tool input object (for Task/Agent: includes `prompt`, `model`, `subagent_type`)
    99	- `tool_result` — the canonical result object (PostToolUse only)
   100	
   101	SessionStart includes:
   102	- `source` — one of `startup`, `resume`, `clear`, `compact`
   103	- `agent_id`, `agent_type`, `permission_mode` — optional fields, null when absent
   104	
   105	### P3 — env-file passthrough pattern (from session-start.sh)
   106	
   107	The env-file passthrough writes shell-safe `export VAR=value` lines to `$CLAUDE_ENV_FILE` so downstream Bash tool calls and subagents can source the file to pick up the exported values.
   108	
   109	Three tiers:
   110	
   111	**REQUIRED fields** — always present in the payload; emit unconditionally:
   112	```bash
   113	jq -r '@sh "export CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID=\(.session_id)"' <<<"$payload" >> "${CLAUDE_ENV_FILE}"
   114	```
   115	
   116	**OPTIONAL fields** — may be null; emit only when non-null:
   117	```bash
   118	jq -r 'if .agent_id != null then @sh "export CLAUDE_AGENT_ID=\(.agent_id)" else empty end' \
   119	    <<<"$payload" >> "${CLAUDE_ENV_FILE}"
   120	```
   121	
   122	**PASSTHROUGH re-exports** — re-export if already set in this process's environment (use `%q` quoting, which is bash-only — safe because the shebang is `#!/usr/bin/env bash`):
   123	```bash
   124	for _var in CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT CLAUDE_PLUGIN_DATA; do
   125	    if [[ -n "${!_var:-}" ]]; then
   126	        printf 'export %s=%q\n' "${_var}" "${!_var}" >> "${CLAUDE_ENV_FILE}"
   127	    fi
   128	done
   129	```
   130	
   131	Note: `session-start.sh` exports `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` from the payload's `session_id` field. In a hook context, `$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` refers to the session Claude Code is running — the parent session for a manager hook, the subagent session for a subagent hook. This is hook-mechanics documentation; do not confuse it with the delegation-prompt `session-id:` field used for file-path construction in agent skills.
   132	
   133	### P4 — agents[] upsert pattern with flock (from post-tool-use-agents.sh)
   134	
   135	The critical section for a read-modify-write on `session.json`:
   136	
   137	```bash
   138	lock_file="$session_json.lock"
   139	tmp_file="$session_json.tmp.$$"
   140	
   141	(
   142	    flock -x 9 || { log "flock failed on $lock_file"; exit 0; }
   143	
   144	    # jq pipeline: upsert by id; atomic mv
   145	    if ! jq --argjson new "$upsert_input" '
   146	        .agents = (
   147	            (.agents // [])
   148	            | (map(.id) | index($new.id)) as $idx
   149	            | if $idx == null
   150	              then . + [ $new + { startedAt: ($new.finishedAt) } ]
   151	              else (.[$idx] | (.startedAt // $new.finishedAt)) as $kept_started
   152	                   | .[0:$idx]
   153	                     + [ .[$idx] + $new + { startedAt: $kept_started } ]
   154	                     + .[$idx+1:]
   155	              end
   156	        )
   157	    ' "$session_json" > "$tmp_file"; then
   158	        log "jq upsert failed"
   159	        rm -f "$tmp_file"
   160	        exit 0
   161	    fi
   162	
   163	    if ! jq -e . "$tmp_file" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
   164	        log "tmp file failed JSON validation"
   165	        rm -f "$tmp_file"
   166	        exit 0
   167	    fi
   168	
   169	    mv -f "$tmp_file" "$session_json"
   170	) 9>"$lock_file"
   171	```
   172	
   173	Key points:
   174	- The `(...)  9>"$lock_file"` subshell holds the lock for the entire read-modify-write.
   175	- `flock -x 9` blocks until the lock is acquired; the `|| { log ...; exit 0; }` guard handles the rare case where flock itself fails.
   176	- Always validate the tmp file with `jq -e .` before `mv` — never replace the original with invalid JSON.
   177	- Use `$$.` in the tmp filename to make it process-unique and avoid collisions from concurrent hook invocations.
   178	
   179	### P5 — two-tier extraction (from post-tool-use-agents.sh)
   180	
   181	When extracting rich result data, prefer the `toolUseResult` from the transcript JSONL (tier 1 — richer, includes `agentId`, `agentType`, `usage`, `totalDurationMs`) and fall back to the `tool_result` from stdin (tier 2 — canonical but leaner):
   182	
   183	```bash
   184	tier1=""
   185	if [[ -n "$transcript_path" && -f "$transcript_path" ]]; then
   186	    tier1=$(jq -c --arg tuid "$tool_use_id" '
   187	        select(.toolUseResult != null)
   188	        | select((.message.content[]?.tool_use_id // empty) == $tuid)
   189	    ' "$transcript_path" 2>/dev/null | tail -n1 || true)
   190	fi
   191	```
   192	
   193	Use `tail -n1` to pick the last matching line — retries supersede earlier attempts. Fall back to `jq -c '.tool_result // null' <<<"$payload"` when tier 1 is empty.
   194	
   195	### P6 — session-dir resolver pattern
   196	
   197	When the hook needs to locate `session.json` given `session_id` and `cwd`, use a two-step resolver:
   198	
   199	1. **Step (i) DORMANT**: read `$cwd/.gobbi/project.json` if it exists; extract `.name`.
   200	2. **Step (ii) ACTIVE**: scan `$cwd/.gobbi/projects/` — if exactly one project directory exists, use its basename. If zero or more than one, `bail`.
   201	
   202	Then scan `$project_dir/sessions/` for a directory whose suffix matches the `session_id` UUID. Require exactly one match; bail if ambiguous.
   203	
   204	### P7 — testing and verification of a hook
   205	
   206	Before merging any hook change:
   207	
   208	1. Run the hook manually with a minimal valid stdin payload. For a SessionStart hook:
   209	   ```bash
   210	   echo '{"session_id":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001","transcript_path":"/tmp/transcript.jsonl","cwd":"/tmp","hook_event_name":"SessionStart","source":"startup"}' \
   211	     | bash .claude/hooks/session-start.sh
   212	   ```
   213	   For a PostToolUse hook:
   214	   ```bash
   215	   echo '{"session_id":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001","transcript_path":"/tmp/transcript.jsonl","cwd":"/tmp","hook_event_name":"PostToolUse","tool_name":"Task","tool_use_id":"tu-001","tool_input":{"prompt":"hello"},"tool_result":{}}' \
   216	     | bash .claude/hooks/post-tool-use-agents.sh
   217	   ```
   218	   Confirm the hook exits 0 and produces the expected output.
   219	2. Verify the hook is registered in `.claude/settings.json` under the correct event and matcher.
   220	3. Start a Claude Code session and trigger the event. Inspect the output file or `session.json` to confirm the hook fired.
   221	4. Test the failure path: pass malformed JSON to stdin, verify the hook exits 0 and logs to stderr without corrupting output files.
   222	5. For `flock`-based hooks: confirm concurrent invocations (two background processes simultaneously) produce a valid merged result.
   223	
   224	---
   225	
   226	## Constraints
   227	
   228	- **MUST always exit 0 in PostToolUse and PostToolUseFailure hooks** — a non-zero exit blocks the tool call that triggered the hook. Log diagnostics to stderr; never abort the hook for recoverable conditions.
   229	- **MUST use `jq -r @sh`** for writing JSON string values into shell `export` lines — never raw string interpolation or printf without quoting.
   230	- **MUST hold `flock -x` for the entire read-modify-write critical section** — acquire the lock at the top of the subshell; release is automatic at subshell exit.
   231	- **MUST validate the tmp file with `jq -e .` before `mv`** — replacing a valid JSON file with invalid JSON is unrecoverable without manual intervention.
   232	- **MUST filter by tool_name** before executing hook logic in PostToolUse/PostToolUseFailure hooks — use a `case` statement; all non-matching tool names exit 0 immediately.
   233	- **MUST use `bail()`** (log to stderr + `exit 0`) for every error condition in PostToolUse/PostToolUseFailure hooks — never `exit 1` from these events.
   234	- **NEVER use `-e` in `set -euo pipefail`** for PostToolUse/PostToolUseFailure hooks — individual `jq` extractions may legitimately fail; `-e` would abort the whole hook on a missing optional field.
   235	- **NEVER stream stdin to multiple `jq` calls** — read `payload="$(cat)"` once at the top; use `<<<"$payload"` for all subsequent `jq` invocations.
   236	- **NEVER write to `$CLAUDE_ENV_FILE` without first checking it is set and writable** — the guard at the top of `session-start.sh` (lines 32-39) is the required pattern.
   237	- **ALWAYS register the hook in `.claude/settings.json`** under the correct event name and matcher — an unregistered hook never fires.
   238	
   239	---
   240	
   241	## Anti-patterns
   242	
   243	- **Forgetting the tool-name filter** — registering a PostToolUse hook for a broad matcher without the `case "$tool_name" in ... esac` filter causes the hook to run on every tool invocation (Read, Write, Edit, Bash, ...) rather than only Task/Agent spawns. Add the filter immediately after stdin parsing.
   244	
   245	- **Raw string quoting in env-file exports** — writing `echo "export MYVAR=$value" >> "$CLAUDE_ENV_FILE"` is fragile if `$value` contains spaces, dollar signs, or single quotes. Always use `jq -r '@sh'` for JSON-sourced values; use `printf '%q'` for values sourced from the shell environment.
   246	
   247	- **Non-atomic file replacement** — writing directly to `session.json` without a tmp-file `mv` risks a partial write if the hook is interrupted mid-write. Always write to a tmp file, validate, then `mv -f`.
   248	
   249	- **Using `set -e` in PostToolUse hooks** — a single non-zero exit from a `jq` extraction (e.g., a missing optional field returns 1 even with `// null`) will abort the hook mid-script and produce an unpredictable partial state. Omit `-e`; use explicit `|| true` on fallible extractions.
   250	
   251	- **Hardcoding session paths** — never hardcode the session UUID or session directory path in hook scripts. Always derive the session directory by resolving from `session_id` + `cwd` via the two-step resolver pattern (P6).
   252	
   253	---
   254	
   255	## Output paths
   256	
   257	Hooks write to files at runtime; the paths below are the live runtime targets, not session staging paths.
   258	
   259	| Output | Path | Written by | Event |
   260	|---|---|---|---|
   261	| Env-var export lines | `$CLAUDE_ENV_FILE` (runtime env; path set by Claude Code) | `session-start.sh` | SessionStart |
   262	| agents[] entry in session.json | `.gobbi/projects/{project}/sessions/{date}-{uuid}/session.json` | `post-tool-use-agents.sh` | PostToolUse / PostToolUseFailure |
   263	
   264	Hook scripts themselves live at:
   265	
   266	| File | Event | Lines |
   267	|---|---|---|
   268	| `.claude/hooks/session-start.sh` | SessionStart | 79 |
   269	| `.claude/hooks/post-tool-use-agents.sh` | PostToolUse + PostToolUseFailure | 251 |
   270	
   271	Registration is in `.claude/settings.json` under the `hooks` key, one array entry per event.

